WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.730
In a French enrichment plant, a sample of ore came up a fraction short of uranium-235 — a shortfall of six hundredths of a percent that should have been impossible,

2
00:00:09.730 --> 00:00:13.700
because every rock in the solar system carries the same ratio.

3
00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:18.150
The explanation was that someone had already burned the missing atoms.

4
00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:24.360
Two billion years before anyone built a reactor, a seam of rock in Gabon had been running one.

5
00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:40.850
In the spring of nineteen seventy-two, in the south of France, at the uranium enrichment plant in the town of Pierrelatte — the French enrichment plant,

6
00:00:40.850 --> 00:00:46.100
for short — a measurement was under way that nobody expected any excitement from.

7
00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:57.750
The raw uranium arriving at the plant moved through the pipes as a gas, uranium hexafluoride, and the proportion of uranium two thirty-five inside it was checked routinely with a mass spectrometer.

8
00:00:57.750 --> 00:01:01.880
This check was less a quality test than an accounting operation.

9
00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:07.930
Because the natural abundance of uranium two thirty-five was held to be as dependable as a constant of nature.

10
00:01:07.930 --> 00:01:12.220
That constant was zero point seven two zero percent.

11
00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:24.510
Ore dug in Australia gave it, so did the shield rocks of Canada, so did the Czech mines, and so did stones brought back from the Moon and meteorites fallen out of the sky: out of every thousand uranium atoms,

12
00:01:24.510 --> 00:01:28.320
roughly seven point two were uranium two thirty-five.

13
00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:31.010
There was an explanation for this.

14
00:01:31.010 --> 00:01:41.220
In the cloud that formed the solar system, the two isotopes of uranium had been blended in a certain ratio, and ever since, each had been decaying at its own pace.

15
00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:48.070
Whichever planet, whichever continent, whichever rock you happened to be standing on, the clock read the same.

16
00:01:48.070 --> 00:01:56.840
Geologists treated that ratio as so solid that when a sample showed a deviation, the first thing that came to mind was not nature but a laboratory error.

17
00:01:56.840 --> 00:02:01.770
That day the screen did not read zero point seven two zero percent.

18
00:02:01.770 --> 00:02:05.580
It read zero point seven one seven.

19
00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:09.550
The difference was zero point zero zero three percent.

20
00:02:09.550 --> 00:02:12.880
On paper, nothing at all.

21
00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:21.650
But the instrument taking the measurement was sensitive enough to separate that difference from noise, and more importantly, the number had not shifted in years.

22
00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:31.220
Henri Bouzigues, on the team that took the reading, first did the expected thing: he checked the instrument, prepared a fresh sample, went over the calibration.

23
00:02:31.220 --> 00:02:33.270
The number would not move.

24
00:02:33.270 --> 00:02:38.680
Then the shipment records were examined, and a far more uncomfortable picture emerged.

25
00:02:38.680 --> 00:02:49.850
Spread across the whole mass of uranium arriving at the plant, that minuscule deviation amounted to a shortfall of about two hundred kilograms of uranium two thirty-five that was simply not there.

26
00:02:49.850 --> 00:03:00.380
In nineteen seventy-two, in the very middle of the Cold War, it is not hard to imagine what the sentence "uranium two thirty-five is missing" meant inside a nuclear fuel facility.

27
00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:05.470
The first scenarios anyone reached for were not nature's but humanity's.

28
00:03:05.470 --> 00:03:08.320
Had someone mixed up the material?

29
00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:15.330
Had uranium that had already been through the fuel cycle, uranium burned in a reactor, been blended somewhere with fresh ore?

30
00:03:15.330 --> 00:03:20.420
Because only one known thing depletes uranium two thirty-five: fission.

31
00:03:20.420 --> 00:03:22.310
A reactor.

32
00:03:22.310 --> 00:03:29.960
A chain reaction consumes uranium two thirty-five and leaves behind a residue enriched in uranium two thirty-eight.

33
00:03:29.960 --> 00:03:36.010
What was showing on the screen was precisely that — the signature of spent fuel.

34
00:03:36.010 --> 00:03:39.820
The investigation traced that batch back to where it had come from.

35
00:03:39.820 --> 00:03:45.870
In the Franceville basin, in the southeast of Gabon, an open-pit mine called Oklo.

36
00:03:45.870 --> 00:03:52.880
The French Atomic Energy Commission sent a team there, and samples began to be taken from the ore veins one by one.

37
00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:58.930
The results coming out of the pit made the deviation at the French enrichment plant look almost trivial.

38
00:03:58.930 --> 00:04:03.700
At some points the ratio dropped to zero point six zero percent.

39
00:04:03.700 --> 00:04:07.830
In one vein it was measured at zero point four four.

40
00:04:07.830 --> 00:04:14.200
That is to say, nearly forty percent of the uranium two thirty-five in that rock was gone.

41
00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:18.970
Nothing like it had ever been seen anywhere in the world, in any ore.

42
00:04:18.970 --> 00:04:25.020
Could a geological process pick out one isotope of uranium from the other and carry it away selectively?

43
00:04:25.020 --> 00:04:35.230
Chemically, uranium two thirty-five and uranium two thirty-eight are identical twins; they form the same compounds, dissolve in the same water, sit in the same mineral.

44
00:04:35.230 --> 00:04:42.720
The only difference between them is the weight of three neutrons, and nature cannot use that difference to achieve a forty percent separation.

45
00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:52.610
To perform that separation, human beings were spending enormous facilities, thousands of stages, kilometres of pipework and as much electricity as a city.

46
00:04:52.610 --> 00:04:56.740
That, after all, was the entire reason the plant existed.

47
00:04:56.740 --> 00:05:02.310
The decisive answer came not from the uranium but from the elements sitting beside it.

48
00:05:02.310 --> 00:05:06.280
The researchers looked at the neodymium in the Oklo ore.

49
00:05:06.280 --> 00:05:14.250
Neodymium in nature is a particular mixture of several isotopes, and about a quarter of that mixture is neodymium one forty-two.

50
00:05:14.250 --> 00:05:21.900
Fission, however, produces neodymium without producing neodymium one forty-two — there is no decay chain leading to that isotope.

51
00:05:21.900 --> 00:05:27.630
In the samples from Oklo, neodymium one forty-two was almost entirely absent.

52
00:05:27.630 --> 00:05:38.320
The remaining isotopes, meanwhile, matched the yield distribution of uranium two thirty-five fission, measured thousands of times in laboratories, almost line for line.

53
00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:41.330
Ruthenium told the same story.

54
00:05:41.330 --> 00:05:44.340
There was reactor ash inside the rock.

55
00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:52.790
On the twenty-fifth of September, nineteen seventy-two, the physicist Francis Perrin announced the result at the French Academy of Sciences.

56
00:05:52.790 --> 00:05:55.640
Nobody had stolen the missing uranium.

57
00:05:55.640 --> 00:06:01.370
At Oklo, long before there were people, a nuclear reactor had run of its own accord.

58
00:06:01.370 --> 00:06:06.780
The strangest thing about this finding was that it was not entirely a surprise.

59
00:06:06.780 --> 00:06:19.390
In nineteen fifty-six, the chemist Paul Kuroda, working at the University of Arkansas, had sat down and done a simple calculation: what conditions would be needed for a chain reaction to start by itself in nature?

60
00:06:19.390 --> 00:06:27.360
He had boiled the answer down to a handful of items, and the conclusion was this — there is a period in the Earth's past when it would have been possible.

61
00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:32.450
Kuroda's paper sat for sixteen years as an elegant but academic curiosity.

62
00:06:32.450 --> 00:06:37.380
Then rock from Gabon turned that calculation from a prediction into a record.

63
00:06:37.380 --> 00:06:43.270
Kuroda's first condition had to do with time, and it is a condition that cannot be met today.

64
00:06:43.270 --> 00:06:53.320
The half-life of uranium two thirty-five is about seven hundred and four million years; that of uranium two thirty-eight is four billion four hundred and seventy million years.

65
00:06:53.320 --> 00:06:58.090
Both are dwindling, but uranium two thirty-five far faster.

66
00:06:58.090 --> 00:07:04.620
Which means that the ratio that stands at zero point seven two percent today grows the further back you go.

67
00:07:04.620 --> 00:07:08.590
A billion years ago it was around one and a half percent.

68
00:07:08.590 --> 00:07:12.560
Roughly two billion years ago it was above three percent.

69
00:07:12.560 --> 00:07:20.050
Note that figure: most commercial power reactors in the world today run on fuel enriched to between three and five percent.

70
00:07:20.050 --> 00:07:27.860
So two billion years ago every uranium vein in the Earth's crust was ready-made reactor fuel, without passing through any process at all.

71
00:07:27.860 --> 00:07:32.950
The world was once made of enriched uranium, and that age closed for good.

72
00:07:32.950 --> 00:07:36.280
But enriched fuel alone is not enough.

73
00:07:36.280 --> 00:07:45.370
The neutrons released in fission are very fast, and fast neutrons do not split uranium two thirty-five efficiently; they have to be slowed down first.

74
00:07:45.370 --> 00:07:52.220
The substance that does this job is called a moderator, and the most common moderator on Earth is water.

75
00:07:52.220 --> 00:08:02.750
When a neutron strikes a hydrogen nucleus of almost exactly its own mass, it gives up most of its energy in a single collision, the way a billiard ball does when it hits its own twin.

76
00:08:02.750 --> 00:08:07.840
The rock at Oklo was porous, and those pores were filled with groundwater.

77
00:08:07.840 --> 00:08:13.250
The vein itself was a reactor core: fuel and moderator, interleaved with one another.

78
00:08:13.250 --> 00:08:17.060
The third condition was cleanliness.

79
00:08:17.060 --> 00:08:25.830
Some elements swallow neutrons and kill the chain before it can even begin; boron, cadmium and certain rare earth elements are foremost among them.

80
00:08:25.830 --> 00:08:31.400
In human-built reactors these substances are deliberately placed in control rods.

81
00:08:31.400 --> 00:08:36.970
In the Oklo veins, as luck would have it, these neutron poisons were scarce.

82
00:08:36.970 --> 00:08:44.620
The fourth condition, and perhaps the most astonishing, concerned how the uranium came to be piled up there in the first place.

83
00:08:44.620 --> 00:08:51.470
For a chain reaction, it is not enough for uranium to be sprinkled through the rock; it has to be concentrated.

84
00:08:51.470 --> 00:08:57.200
In some of Oklo's reactor veins the uranium content of the ore exceeded twenty percent.

85
00:08:57.200 --> 00:09:04.210
What produced that concentration was water, but for water to carry uranium there had to be oxygen in the atmosphere.

86
00:09:04.210 --> 00:09:09.460
In an oxygen-free world uranium does not dissolve; it stays where it is.

87
00:09:09.460 --> 00:09:21.590
About two billion four hundred million years ago, when photosynthesising microbes permanently altered the atmosphere — during the period known as the Great Oxidation Event — uranium became soluble.

88
00:09:21.590 --> 00:09:28.920
Groundwater began stripping it out of the rock, carrying it, and precipitating it again in sediments rich in organic matter.

89
00:09:28.920 --> 00:09:34.650
In other words, one of the things that made the Oklo reactor possible was living creatures.

90
00:09:34.650 --> 00:09:38.780
Bacteria had stockpiled the fuel for the world's first nuclear plant.

91
00:09:38.780 --> 00:09:49.150
These four conditions came together roughly one billion seven hundred million to two billion years ago, in those few veins of the Oklo basin, and the reaction began on its own.

92
00:09:49.150 --> 00:10:00.480
In the same region, sixteen reactor zones were found at Oklo itself, one at the immediately adjacent Okélobondo deposit, and one more at Bangombé, about thirty kilometres to the south:

93
00:10:00.480 --> 00:10:03.650
seventeen separate natural reactors in all.

94
00:10:03.650 --> 00:10:07.140
So why did this thing never run away?

95
00:10:07.140 --> 00:10:12.230
The most dangerous property of a chain reaction is that it feeds itself and grows.

96
00:10:12.230 --> 00:10:16.680
Oklo had its own brake, and that brake was the water inside the fuel.

97
00:10:16.680 --> 00:10:22.890
When the reaction started, the rock heated up, and the water in the pores boiled and turned to vapour.

98
00:10:22.890 --> 00:10:30.380
With the water gone, the moderator went too; the neutrons stayed fast, the chain broke, the reactor fell silent.

99
00:10:30.380 --> 00:10:38.670
As the rock cooled, groundwater seeped back into the pores, the moderator returned to its place, and the reaction reignited.

100
00:10:38.670 --> 00:10:43.120
A reactor that breathed in and out, like a geyser erupting and resting.

101
00:10:43.120 --> 00:10:46.130
Even the rhythm of that cycle has been measured.

102
00:10:46.130 --> 00:10:58.260
In two thousand and four, Alex Meshik and his colleagues used a laser to release, one at a time, the xenon isotopes trapped inside aluminium phosphate minerals in the reactor veins, and studied them.

103
00:10:58.260 --> 00:11:10.710
Xenon is a product of fission, and different xenon isotopes form over different timescales; so the isotope pattern recorded inside the mineral gives away how long the reactor stayed on and how long it stayed off.

104
00:11:10.710 --> 00:11:18.040
The result was this: roughly thirty minutes of operation, followed by roughly two and a half hours of silence.

105
00:11:18.040 --> 00:11:20.090
Then thirty minutes again.

106
00:11:20.090 --> 00:11:21.980
A three-hour pulse.

107
00:11:21.980 --> 00:11:26.750
That pulse beat, on and off, for several hundred thousand years.

108
00:11:26.750 --> 00:11:34.720
Its average power was modest; thermally it probably never exceeded one hundred kilowatts, enough to feed a few dozen toasters.

109
00:11:34.720 --> 00:11:43.650
But the span was so long that in total about five tonnes of uranium two thirty-five underwent fission, and the rock heated to several hundred degrees.

110
00:11:43.650 --> 00:11:50.500
That deviation of a few parts per thousand at the French enrichment plant was the leftover arithmetic of those five tonnes.

111
00:11:50.500 --> 00:11:56.710
The real value of Oklo lies not in how strange a coincidence it was, but in what it left behind.

112
00:11:56.710 --> 00:11:59.400
Every reactor produces waste.

113
00:11:59.400 --> 00:12:07.370
Oklo produced it too: strontium, caesium, technetium, iodine, rare earth elements and actinides.

114
00:12:07.370 --> 00:12:18.700
Some of the neutrons generated in the veins were absorbed by uranium two thirty-eight and turned into plutonium two thirty-nine; part of that plutonium fissioned where it stood, and the rest decayed,

115
00:12:18.700 --> 00:12:23.790
with its twenty-four-thousand-year half-life, back into uranium two thirty-five.

116
00:12:23.790 --> 00:12:28.400
Oklo, in other words, was also a plutonium factory.

117
00:12:28.400 --> 00:12:32.530
Now the real question: where did all that material go?

118
00:12:32.530 --> 00:12:35.860
There was no sealed steel vessel over it.

119
00:12:35.860 --> 00:12:38.070
No concrete.

120
00:12:38.070 --> 00:12:43.640
No glass matrix, no copper canister, no engineered barrier.

121
00:12:43.640 --> 00:12:49.210
There was only rock, and groundwater flowed through that rock for two billion years.

122
00:12:49.210 --> 00:12:58.300
The answer is one that everybody debating nuclear waste storage ought to stop and think about: most of the non-volatile fission products and the actinides barely stirred at all.

123
00:12:58.300 --> 00:13:04.990
Looking at how they are distributed through the veins, their movement is measured in centimetres, a few metres at most.

124
00:13:04.990 --> 00:13:08.160
Plutonium decayed where it was produced.

125
00:13:08.160 --> 00:13:13.410
The rare earth elements worked their way into the mineral structure of the ore and locked themselves there.

126
00:13:13.410 --> 00:13:22.180
We know that even a noble gas like xenon was held inside phosphate minerals for billions of years, because it was from that xenon that we read the reactor's pulse.

127
00:13:22.180 --> 00:13:28.710
This is why Oklo is invoked today, in discussions of geological disposal, as a natural analogue.

128
00:13:28.710 --> 00:13:38.120
In a laboratory you can test how well a mineral holds something for a few years at most; from there you make a projection reaching a hundred thousand years into the future,

129
00:13:38.120 --> 00:13:42.410
and nobody will ever be alive to see whether the projection was right.

130
00:13:42.410 --> 00:13:47.660
Oklo has already run that experiment and written the result into the rock.

131
00:13:47.660 --> 00:13:56.110
It would be wrong, however, to draw from this the conclusion that nuclear waste simply stays put by itself, because the outcome is not the same everywhere.

132
00:13:56.110 --> 00:14:04.560
At Bangombé, the zone closest to the surface, uranium and some of the products can be seen to have migrated a certain distance into the surroundings.

133
00:14:04.560 --> 00:14:13.810
The difference comes less from chemistry than from earth science: depth, the impermeability of the overlying rock, the chemistry of the water, the presence of clay.

134
00:14:13.810 --> 00:14:19.860
What Oklo says is not "waste is safe"; it is "you can measure which geology holds and which one leaks."

135
00:14:19.860 --> 00:14:24.790
The record in that rock also found an entirely different kind of reader.

136
00:14:24.790 --> 00:14:33.880
Whether the fundamental constants of physics are truly constant has long been argued over; there are theories proposing that the number known as the fine-structure constant,

137
00:14:33.880 --> 00:14:40.410
which sets the strength of the electromagnetic interaction, may have changed very slowly across the ages.

138
00:14:40.410 --> 00:14:45.500
To test this you need a sensitive nuclear apparatus that has been running for billions of years.

139
00:14:45.500 --> 00:14:48.030
Oklo is exactly that.

140
00:14:48.030 --> 00:14:56.160
The probability that the isotope samarium one forty-nine absorbs a neutron rises to a sharp peak across a very narrow band of energy,

141
00:14:56.160 --> 00:15:02.210
and the position of that peak is sensitive to even the slightest shift in the fine-structure constant.

142
00:15:02.210 --> 00:15:10.660
Had the constant been a little different, the proportions of the samarium isotopes remaining in the Oklo ore would have come out noticeably different from what they are.

143
00:15:10.660 --> 00:15:19.110
The measurements were made, and the result showed that the constant has stayed the same, within an extraordinarily tight limit, for two billion years.

144
00:15:19.110 --> 00:15:26.280
A mine in Gabon has given us one of the firmest pieces of evidence we currently hold that the rules of the universe do not change.

145
00:15:26.280 --> 00:15:33.770
The Oklo pit itself no longer operates; the ore was extracted, and most of the zones disappeared along with the mining.

146
00:15:33.770 --> 00:15:39.980
A few veins have been placed under protection by Gabon for their scientific value.

147
00:15:39.980 --> 00:15:50.830
Looking back, we can see that the first controlled chain reaction, assembled in December of nineteen forty-two beneath a sports hall in Chicago out of graphite bricks and cubes of uranium,

148
00:15:50.830 --> 00:15:54.160
was not really an invention but a rediscovery.

149
00:15:54.160 --> 00:16:04.050
The same physics, the same logic of moderation, the same self-braking balance had been set up spontaneously by the Earth's crust in Gabon two billion years earlier.

150
00:16:04.050 --> 00:16:11.060
The difference is that nature did not do it on purpose; the right rock, the right water and the right age simply came together.

151
00:16:11.060 --> 00:16:17.430
And we know all of this thanks to a deviation of three parts in a thousand that appeared on a screen in a laboratory.

152
00:16:17.430 --> 00:16:22.680
The missing uranium was the mark not of a theft, but of the world's own past.

153
00:16:22.680 --> 00:16:30.010
Had the team taking that measurement shrugged that day and said the instrument must be broken, the rock would have gone on keeping its silence.

154
00:16:30.010 --> 00:16:33.500
Nöron Science'de bugünkü bölümün sonuna geldik.

155
00:16:33.500 --> 00:16:36.830
Yeni bir hikâyede yeniden buluşmak üzere.
